data.content:
## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** patterns that break on .NET 8:

| API | Line | Pattern | Problem on .NET 8 |
|-----|------|---------|--------------------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Stop()` | Blocking call interruption | `SYSLIB0046` (obsolete in .NET 9, unreliable in .NET 8) |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `Stop()` | Forcible termination fallback | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `RunLoop()` | Uninterruptible block | Can't be woken by cancellation token |

There's also a **race condition bug**: multiple items can be enqueued before `WaitOne` returns, but only one is dequeued per signal — remaining items are lost.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle };

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny wakes on either a new event OR cancellation
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            // Drain all queued items (fixes the original race condition)
            List<string> items;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                items = new List<string>(_queue);
                _queue.Clear();
            }

            foreach (var item in items)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();            // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);      // wait for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes

| What changed | Why |
|---|---|
| `CancellationTokenSource` replaces both `Thread.Interrupt` and `Thread.Abort` | Cooperative cancellation — the only model .NET 8 supports |
| `WaitHandle.WaitAny(event, token.WaitHandle)` replaces bare `WaitOne()` | Wakes the thread on **either** a new item or cancellation |
| `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` replaces `while (true)` | Loop exits cleanly when cancelled |
| `ManualResetEventSlim` replaces `ManualResetEvent` | Lighter weight; spins briefly before kernel wait |
| Drain loop instead of single `Dequeue` | Fixes race where multiple `Enqueue` calls before `WaitOne` returns lost items |
| `IDisposable` added | `CancellationTokenSource` and `ManualResetEventSlim` must be disposed to avoid timer/handle leaks |

## Pitfalls to Watch

1. **`Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — This is the hard blocker. There is no force-kill equivalent in .NET 8. If `Handle(item)` hangs, cancellation won't interrupt it mid-execution. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints inside `Handle` if it does long-running work.

2. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — If you convert to `async`/`Task.Run` later, let `OperationCanceledException` propagate up the call chain.

3. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — Leaking it causes timer and callback leaks. Always wrap the owner in `IDisposable`.

4. **`Stop()` has no force-kill fallback** — The old code had `Abort()` as a safety net after `Join(5000)`. In .NET 8, if the thread doesn't exit in 5 seconds, you must either accept the hang or move uncooperative work to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()`.
